Perhaps you can just empty the default plot hook: knit_hooks$set(plot<br>= function(x, options) &#39;&#39;)<br><br>Regards,<br>Yihui<br>--<br>Yihui Xie &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel="nofollow">xiey...@gmail.com</a>&gt;<br>Phone: <a href="tel:(515)%20294-2465" value="+15152942465" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">515-294-2465</a> Web: <a href="http://yihui.name" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://yihui.name&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792356248561000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3BvdOdeO7mHm8B6WJFOpeU">http://yihui.name</a><br>Department of Statistics, Iowa State University<br>2215 Snedecor Hall, Ames, IA<br><br><br>On Wed, Oct 10, 2012 at 7:47 PM, Peter Gallagher &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel="nofollow">pwgal...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>&gt; I&#39;m trying to use the LaTeX &quot;mdframed&quot; package to frame a plot inside Knitr<br>&gt; (LaTeX). The mdframed package is using TikZ to create a nice rounded corners<br>&gt; frame.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Because the mdframed environment disrupts the float, it has to be called<br>&gt; INSIDE LaTeX&#39;s  igure{} environment or LaTeX complains that I&#39;m calling a<br>&gt; float (the figure) while not in &#39;outer par&#39; mode.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; So I&#39;m using a knitr_hook to add the environment calls either side of the<br>&gt; \includegraphics command, like this:<br>&gt;<br>&gt; knit_hooks$set(roundFrame = function(before, options, envir) {<br>&gt;    if (before) {<br>&gt;   name = paste(options$fig.path, options$label, sep = &#39;&#39;)<br>&gt;   return(paste(&#39;\begin{mdframed}[roundcorner=7pt] 
 \includegraphics{&#39;,<br>&gt; name, &#39;} 
 \end{mdframed} 
&#39;, sep = &#39;&#39;))<br>&gt;      } })<br>&gt;<br>&gt; That seems to work (although I can&#39;t seem to control the output width of the<br>&gt; r.plot).<br>&gt;<br>&gt; My main problem is that the knitr chunk outputs this version of the plot and<br>&gt; then ALSO OUTPUTS the standard version of the plot (without the frame).<br>&gt;<br>&gt; The chunk looks like this:<br>&gt;<br>&gt; %% begin.rcode my_graph, cache=FALSE, fig.cap=&#39;My beautiful graph&#39;,<br>&gt; out.width=&#39;0.7\linewidth&#39;, fig.show=&#39;hold&#39;, roundFrame=TRUE<br>&gt; % r_function_that_calls_and_prints_ a_ggplot2_graphic()<br>&gt; %% end.rcode<br>&gt;<br>&gt; How can I stop the chunk DUPLICATING the graphic, once with and once without<br>&gt; the frame?<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>&gt;<br>
